# Arcade Color and Sound Plan

## Module structure

- Keep visual tokens and motion in `src/styles.css`, with matching semantic
  color names in `tailwind.config.ts` when component utility classes need them.
- Keep animated ambient presentation at the app shell (`App.tsx`) so it spans
  every screen without participating in game state or collision rendering.
- Keep falling-block color selection in the game rendering layer. Derive a
  stable palette entry from each block's existing id so colors are visual-only
  and never change spawning, movement, collision, or testable block state.
- Add sound modules under `src/audio/`: a small browser-safe Web Audio service,
  a background-music hook, a victory-tune function, and a pure sound-settings
  utility. Reuse the existing Web Audio compatibility/error-handling approach
  in `src/utils/gameOverEffect.ts` rather than adding audio dependencies or
  shipping binary assets.
- Hold the persisted sound preference at the app shell and pass it to the
  sound hooks and the visible toggle. Keep it outside `MatchState` because it
  is an app preference, not per-match progression.

## Issue order and dependencies

1. **#63** establishes the bright arcade design tokens and updates base
   controls so later visual work uses one palette.
2. **#64** builds the animated app-shell background on those tokens, honoring
   `prefers-reduced-motion` and remaining behind interactive content.
3. **#65** applies the palette to `FallingBlock` with stable id-derived colors;
   gameplay hooks and collision data stay unchanged.
4. **#66** refreshes the screen components and `MatchProgressPanel` using the
   shared tokens so the play area and scoreboard remain readable at narrow
   widths.
5. **#67** adds pause-aware background music while a turn is actively playing.
6. **#68** adds a one-shot winner/tie result tune without replaying on ordinary
   rerenders.
7. **#69** introduces the shared sound on/off preference and a keyboard-safe
   visible toggle, then wires both #67 and #68 to it.
8. **#70** tests the pure preference reducer/serialization behavior and the
   existing game-flow integration points rather than browser audio output.

## Key design decisions

- Preserve all existing match, timer, movement, spawn, and collision state;
  palette/background work is strictly presentational.
- Use CSS custom properties for palette consistency, animated gradients, and
  contrast-friendly foreground colors. Provide a reduced-motion fallback that
  shows the static arcade palette with no continuous animation.
- Use generated Web Audio tones instead of external music files. Audio calls
  must no-op safely when Web Audio is unavailable or autoplay is blocked, and
  contexts/nodes must be stopped and closed during cleanup.
- Background music starts only for `screen === 'playing'`, stops on pause,
  round end, exit, and unmount, and never affects timer progression.
- Victory audio is keyed to a completed results outcome so it plays once per
  result session; ties receive a neutral short cue.
- Default sound to enabled, persist only the boolean preference in
  `localStorage` behind guarded helpers, and use a pure default/fallback path
  for SSR, malformed storage, and tests.
- The sound toggle uses a semantic pressed state and focus-visible styling;
  it must not capture arrow-key gameplay controls.
